WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:04.728
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.728 --> 00:00:07.571
We are twenty months
from the 2020 election,

00:00:07.571 --> 00:00:11.580
what if you heard from
the media about the candidates?

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:15.240
Maybe you've heard about how likable
they are or the odds they can win.

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:20.220
You probably haven't heard much about
their plans to balance the budget.

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:25.541
This far out from the election,
why are we even talking about polls,

00:00:25.541 --> 00:00:27.697
why aren't we talking about policy?

00:00:29.976 --> 00:00:31.554
On May 26, 2016,

00:00:31.554 --> 00:00:36.148
I was working on a presidential
campaign in our headquarter's office.

00:00:36.148 --> 00:00:40.577
I was sitting at my desk working on
briefing memos, the staff was excited that

00:00:40.577 --> 00:00:45.211
day because at 2 o'clock our candidate
was scheduled to give a big policy speech

00:00:45.211 --> 00:00:49.050
about workers rights in Las Vegas,
so we all wanted to watch on TV.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:55.910
As 2 o'clock approached a group of
the staff peeled away from their work and

00:00:55.910 --> 00:00:59.712
huddled around office TVs which
was set to all the major networks.

00:00:59.712 --> 00:01:05.108
The clock ticked down, 1:58, 1:59, but

00:01:05.108 --> 00:01:11.170
then 2 o'clock came and went, and none of
the network showed that policy speech.

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:17.120
Instead, all of them showed that, that's
a live feed of an empty podium at another

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:23.160
candidate's rally in North Dakota, waiting
for that candidate to take the stage.

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:25.700
That cadidate was so great for

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:30.076
ratings that even that image
drew people to the TV.

00:01:30.076 --> 00:01:31.372
It was in that moment,

00:01:31.372 --> 00:01:35.210
I thought there's a crisis with
political media in this country.

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:40.950
It's not a republican democrat crisis,
it's an American problem.

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:45.800
It's a problem of the trivial in our
political media, where personality,

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:48.750
scandal and polling dominate the coverage,

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:51.080
because that's what generates ratings and
profit.

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:54.138
I thought in that moment,

00:01:54.138 --> 00:01:57.736
it's time to rethink the model
of corporate political media.

00:02:00.230 --> 00:02:02.086
Think back to the 2016 election,

00:02:02.086 --> 00:02:05.980
what's the first news story that comes
to mind for you about either candidate?

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:11.245
Raise your hand if that story was about
policy, like economic or foreign policy.

00:02:13.209 --> 00:02:16.658
Now raise your hand if that story
was about personality, scandal, or

00:02:16.658 --> 00:02:18.890
polling, like emails or Tweets, exactly.

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:25.063
In the 2016 election according to Harvard
Kennedy School, 10% of the coverage was

00:02:25.063 --> 00:02:29.279
about policy, two-thirds was about
personality, scandal and polling.

00:02:29.279 --> 00:02:33.690
The coverage of our elections has
become a reality show, and as a viewer,

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:36.790
I'm part of the problem, we all are.

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:42.660
It's hard to turn off the TV or not click
on the headline when it's sensationalized.

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:48.964
But imagine for a moment if our political
media were free from profit pressures,

00:02:48.964 --> 00:02:53.797
free to focus only on the stories they
think the public really needs to know,

00:02:53.797 --> 00:02:57.744
and free to pursue those stories
with depth and with context.

00:03:00.554 --> 00:03:04.601
Over the last few weeks, I've been looking
at headlines about the 2020 candidates in

00:03:04.601 --> 00:03:08.239
some of the leading news sites, here is
the sampling of some of those headlines.

00:03:10.133 --> 00:03:14.017
Kamala Harris' father criticizes her for
marijuana joke,

00:03:14.017 --> 00:03:18.830
Elizabeth Warren drinks a beer on
Instagram live and gets mixed reaction.

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:25.402
Grass roots not grass feed, Cory Booker
could be our first vegan president.

00:03:25.402 --> 00:03:28.910
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; In the pale of winter,

00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:31.405
Trump's tan remains a state secret.

00:03:31.405 --> 00:03:35.190
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; What about TV,

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:39.280
if you turn on cable news in primetime,
what do you see?

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:43.339
It's probably one of two things,
it could be an opinion show,

00:03:43.339 --> 00:03:48.711
where you have a partisan host delivering
a cross between an op-ed and a newscast.

00:03:48.711 --> 00:03:50.318
Or it could be a news show,

00:03:50.318 --> 00:03:54.484
where you probably have a split-screen
with a pundit on the left and

00:03:54.484 --> 00:03:59.620
one on the right, talking past each other
about the hot new controversy of the day.

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:06.160
At the bottom of the screen in capital
letters it probably says, breaking news.

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:10.732
It's the same breaking news headline
whether there's been a national disaster

00:04:10.732 --> 00:04:13.360
or if a politician made
a minor speaking geff.

00:04:16.276 --> 00:04:19.040
So why should you care about this,
and why now?

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:25.420
Because reliable information is
an anchor of a functioning democracy and

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:28.310
we're drifting further and
further from that anchor.

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:33.750
Let's think about the situation we're in,
Americans are increasingly

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:36.930
uninformed three quarters can't name
the three branches of government.

00:04:38.270 --> 00:04:41.560
On top of that you've all
heard about fake news,

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:45.950
there's a blurring of the line between
truth and fiction in our news ecosystem.

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:52.447
An MIT study this year found that
false news stories are 70% more

00:04:52.447 --> 00:04:55.950
likely to get retweeted
than real new stories.

00:04:55.950 --> 00:05:00.687
There's even an emerging AI that can mass
produce fake news making it that much

00:05:00.687 --> 00:05:02.640
harder to detect and mitigate.

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:08.574
And finally less than half of
the Americans trust the mass

00:05:08.574 --> 00:05:12.600
media that's down from 70% in the 1970s.

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:17.237
In this context what will happen in
the next national security emergency,

00:05:17.237 --> 00:05:19.309
how we all agree on what's true?

00:05:22.467 --> 00:05:26.652
We take for granted that our political
media should be run, that it's ideal for

00:05:26.652 --> 00:05:29.950
it to be run as subsidiaries
of corporate giants.

00:05:29.950 --> 00:05:33.720
But that system is largely failing
at what its core mission should be,

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:35.350
to inform and educate.

00:05:36.650 --> 00:05:40.660
What if we considered our political
media to be a public good?

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:44.160
Set aside as something different from
just another product in our economy.

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:50.070
Quality information is essential for
people to make informed decisions,

00:05:50.070 --> 00:05:52.552
it has benefits to our whole society.

00:05:52.552 --> 00:05:57.900
There are already news nonprofits doing
great work like ProPublica and NPR.

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:03.310
And you know what the most trusted
institution in the entire country is?

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:06.674
PBS, we could take that model and

00:06:06.674 --> 00:06:12.572
build a new generation of
political media non-profits.

00:06:12.572 --> 00:06:16.545
With a strictly non-partisan
educational mission, and

00:06:16.545 --> 00:06:21.660
with stable long term capital backing
from foundations and small donors.

00:06:23.762 --> 00:06:28.166
One day maybe yield run a company
that owns media assets,

00:06:28.166 --> 00:06:31.846
maybe you will be looking to invest for
impact.

00:06:31.846 --> 00:06:35.391
When you're in that position consider
the media as part of life blood of

00:06:35.391 --> 00:06:36.229
our democracy.

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:38.660
If you can be a force for

00:06:38.660 --> 00:06:42.910
hiring journalists with
a not-for-profit nonpartisan mission.

00:06:42.910 --> 00:06:44.810
When news rooms are getting dessimated and

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:48.440
pushed towards the trivial,
that's having impact.

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:51.876
And if you work at a social media company,
you can help partner

00:06:51.876 --> 00:06:56.255
with quality news organizations rather
than sidelining and replacing them.

00:06:58.690 --> 00:07:03.867
These are all long term ideas,
how can you actually help today?

00:07:03.867 --> 00:07:09.790
It's not realistic to resolve that we'll
stop consuming infotainment all together.

00:07:09.790 --> 00:07:14.589
But when we do consume it we could remind
ourselves that it's entertainment,

00:07:14.589 --> 00:07:16.634
not a replacement for real news.

00:07:18.849 --> 00:07:23.184
The Washington Post columnist Margaret
Sullivan has said that in an election,

00:07:23.184 --> 00:07:26.482
the media treats policy like
a helping of steamed broccoli,

00:07:26.482 --> 00:07:28.240
shoved to the side of the plate.

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:33.430
Broccoli gets a bad rap but
it's healthy and it's really important for

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:37.770
a balanced diet, maybe we can
make a small pledge here today.

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:44.456
For every few stories we consume between
now and 2020 about the political horse

00:07:44.456 --> 00:07:49.254
race, we could try to find and real and
maybe even share a story about policy.

00:07:52.392 --> 00:07:54.934
In the strongest democracy in the world,

00:07:54.934 --> 00:07:57.920
we can build a new landscape
of political media.

00:07:59.060 --> 00:08:04.667
A media we can be proud off that rises
above the fray to deliver substance and

00:08:04.667 --> 00:08:05.472
context.

00:08:08.653 --> 00:08:11.193
You can help make that a reality,
thank you.

00:08:11.193 --> 00:08:15.474
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:08:15.474 --> 00:08:20.366
[MUSIC]

